UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 17, Lesson 2: Listening and speaking activities', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Active listening means looking at the speaker, keeping still, not interrupting and then showing you understood by saying back the main idea. Clear speaking uses a steady pace, an audible voice and one idea at a time. Games such as ''Describe and draw'' or ''Repeat the message'' build both skills. Everyone has a right to speak and a right to be listened to.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite one active listening sentence you could say after a friend tells you about their weekend.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlay ''Describe and draw'' or ''Repeat the message'' with a family member. Record what was easy, what was tricky and two tips for clearer speaking and better listening. Swap roles and repeat.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Active listening means looking at the speaker, keeping still, not interrupting and then showing you understood by saying back the main idea. Clear speaking uses a steady pace, an audible voice and one idea at a time. Games such as ''Describe and draw'' or ''Repeat the message'' build both skills. Everyone has a right to speak and a right to be listened to.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Play ''Describe and draw'' or ''Repeat the message'' with a family member. Record what was easy, what was tricky and two tips for clearer speaking and better listening. Swap roles and repeat. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it. Reasoning: I used active listening by attending to clear speaking rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood. Correction: Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it. Why: the correction restores accurate use of active listening.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating active listening on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with active listening, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it. This matches the lesson skill for active listening.","options":["Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it.","Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Write one active listening sentence you could say after a friend tells you about their weekend. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of clear speaking with clear evidence.","Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood.","Active listening means looking at the speaker, keeping still, not interrupting and then showing you understood by saying back the main idea. Clear speaking…","Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising active listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named clear speaking, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the clear speaking used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"A learner is practising active listening. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it. uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about active listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about active listening.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about clear speaking?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example ''Describe and draw'': Partner A describes a simple picture (''a big sun in the top corner, two clouds below it'') while Partner B…"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Active listening means looking at the speaker, keeping still, not interrupting and then showing you… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood.","Play ''Describe and draw'' or ''Repeat the message'' with a family member. Record what was easy, what was tricky and two tips for clearer speaking and…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of active listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating active listening on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it. Reasoning: I used active listening by attending to clear speaking rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with active listening, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood. Correction: Sample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it. Why: the correction restores accurate use of active listening.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w17-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise active listening.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use active listening with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify clear speaking and explain why it matters.","I can practise taking turns independently on a new prompt.","I can notice repeating the main idea needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Active listening means looking at the speaker, keeping still, not interrupting and then showing you understood by saying back the main idea. Clear speaking uses a steady pace, an audible voice and one idea at a time. Games such as ''Describe and draw'' or ''Repeat the message''…","options":["Active listening means looking at the speaker, keeping still, not interrupting and then showing you understood by saying back the main idea. Clear speaking uses a steady pace, an audible voice and one idea at a time. Games such as ''Describe and draw'' or ''Repeat the message'' build both skills. Everyone has a right to speak and a right to be listened to.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about active listening?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample ''Describe and draw'': Partner A describes a simple picture (''a big sun in the top corner, two clouds below it'') while Partner B draws without seeing it. They compare and talk about which words were clear. B uses an active listening sentence: ''You said the sun is top right, is that right?''\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite one active listening sentence you could say after a friend tells you about their weekend.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''It sounds like you enjoyed the beach and building a sandcastle. Did the waves wash it away?'' It repeats the main idea and asks a question about it.","explain":"Active listening means looking at the speaker, keeping still, not interrupting and then showing you understood by saying back the main idea. Clear speaking uses a steady pace, an audible voice and one idea at a time. Games such as ''Describe and draw'' or ''Repeat the message'' build both skills. Everyone has a right to speak and a right to be listened to.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking listening is just being quiet. Active listening includes looking, thinking and showing that you understood. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Active listening steps.","caption":"Say the main idea back.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Look</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">eyes on speaker</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Repeat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">you said the sun is top right</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example ''Describe and draw'': Partner A describes a simple picture (''a big sun in the top corner, two clouds below it'') w Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here.